<Base title="IMA" path="/about" description="About the Institute of Musicology and Arts (IMA) at the University of Luxembourg: history, mission and collaborations.">
  <header class="page-head"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">IMA</p><h1>About the <em>institute</em></h1><p class="lead">Part of the Department of Education and Social Work, Faculty of Humanities, Education and Social Sciences, University of Luxembourg.</p></div></header>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap two-col">
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p class="lead">The Institute of Musicology and the Arts is embedded in the Luxembourgish context, where music education is valued as a cornerstone of the education system. Through its research and teaching (Bachelor en Enseignement Musical, IFEN), the institute contributes to music education in Luxembourg, and through collaborations, events and publications to the international body of work on music education.</p>
        <p>The institute was created in 2021. Under the leadership of Prof. Damien Sagrillo, it made a significant contribution to the valorisation of Luxembourg’s musical heritage. The critical edition of the works of the Luxembourgish composer Laurent Menager and the <i>Luxemburger Musikerlexikon</i> stand out.</p>
        <p>In 2022, Prof. Luc Nijs joined the institute, connecting to the institute’s work on music pedagogy and broadening its scope with research on instrumental music teaching in early childhood (KiMuPe), digitisation in music education (Vimus) and music performance (SAMuSe).</p>
        <p>Since May 2026, Prof. Nijs is head of the institute. The institute now focuses on music education and performance research, aiming to become a leading centre for music research on embodied music pedagogy, musical creativity, the musician-instrument relationship, music educational technology and well-being through music.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <h2 style="font-size:30px;margin-bottom:18px">Collaborations</h2>
